<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1867"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Murco<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Murco<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, December 14, 2004 - 2:15 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Some thoughts on ANDERSON: <BR> <BR><a href="http://www.kansas.com/mld/kansas/news/special_packages/btk/history/8312919.htm" target=_top>http://www.kansas.com/mld/kansas/news/special_pack ages/btk/history/8312919.htm</a> <BR>&#34;The last multiple slaying in Wichita occurred last July 1 when a 67-year-old Wichita man allegedly shot and killed his wife, brother and sister-in-law. <BR> <BR> <BR>The suspect, Floyd E. Anderson, died in September at Larned State Hospital while being held for a mental examination. <BR> <BR> <BR>Death was apparently due to a heart attack. <BR> <BR> <BR>Victims of the triple murder were Margaret A. Anderson, 67, wife of the suspect, and Leonard L. Anderson, 60, and his wife, Ruth Anderson. <BR> <BR> <BR>All were shot with a .38 caliber revolver.&#34;<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1867-->
<!--Post: 1870--><!--Time: 1103063617-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1870"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Murco<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Murco<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, December 14, 2004 - 2:33 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I don&#39;t remember which puzzle,  <BR>but one of them had &#34;CPR&#34;.  <BR> <BR>but it got me thinking, and i wonder if he resuscitated <BR> any victims, only to strangle them all over again as part of the &#34;torture&#34;? <BR> <BR>&#34;1960 - Cardiac Massage <BR> <BR> <BR>The next major step in resuscitation was closed chest massage which was introduced in the 1960&#39;s by Dr. Kowenhoven, The crucial aspect of this technique is that the patient receives oxygen which is transported to the brain by the development of a minimal blood circulation. On this basis many national and international guidelines to perform CPR came out. <BR> <BR> <BR> <BR> <BR>1973 - CPR practice for the population <BR> <BR> <BR>During the Vietnam War the US army introduced CPR to the people for the first time. Then, in 1973 the American Red Cross and the American Heart Association &#40;AHA&#41; began a big campaign to teach the American population this method.&#34; <BR> <BR><a href="http://www.ukdivers.net/history/cpr.htm" target=_top>http://www.ukdivers.net/history/cpr.htm</a> <BR> <BR> <BR>did any of the victims take cpr courses? <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1870-->
<!--Post: 2854--><!--Time: 1104199608-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2854"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Bojo<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bojo<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, December 27, 2004 - 6:06 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->With the inference by Wichitaboy regarding the medical school, could &#34;SCHOOL&#34; actually be &#34;RNSCHOOL&#34;? <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2854-->
<!--Post: 2866--><!--Time: 1104209794-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2866"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Wichitaboy<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Wichitaboy<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, December 27, 2004 - 8:56 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I thought about RNSCHOOL, but I discounted it because going down there was QWERT filler.  I thought it was too much coincidence that the R would be used twice.  But who knows? <BR> <BR>Mark<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2866-->
<!--Post: 2876--><!--Time: 1104216657-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2876"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Dawgar<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Dawgar<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, December 27, 2004 - 10:50 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text--><a href="http://images.google.dk/images?q=bdmonkeys/notebook&amp;hl=en&amp;lr=&amp;start=0&amp;sa=N&amp;filter=0" target=_top>http://images.google.dk/images?q=bdmonkeys/noteboo k&amp;hl=en&amp;lr=&amp;start=0&amp;sa=N&amp;filter=0</a> <BR> <BR>this takes you to a very strange set of notes...any thoughts on a btk link?...look at pg 74 and the zodiac drawing<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2876-->
<!--Post: 3422--><!--Time: 1104886713-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3422"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Bojo<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bojo<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, January 04, 2005 - 4:58 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->In the RUSE puzzle &#34;WENDIE&#34; is found.  R36-C6.  It&#39;s an unusaul spelling of Wendy but by no means unheard of.  Coincidence or significant?  The name of his &#34;LOSTDOG&#34; jumps to mind or instead of &#34;REMODEL&#34; it could be &#34;MODEL&#34; and &#34;WENDIE&#34; is her name.  We know he likes photography, could he have used this as part of his &#34;RUSE&#34;?  While I was young at the time, wasn&#39;t Wendie Malick gaining fame? Could he have said he discovered her to gain the trust of one or more of the victims? I know it&#39;s out of the box thinking but just thought I would throw it out there.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3422-->
<!--Post: 3630--><!--Time: 1105073926-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3630"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Jim44095<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Jim44095<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 06, 2005 - 8:58 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Something about the words school and officer struck me as maybe a Truant Officer,who else would know that the Otero children were home sick that day?Just can&#39;t figure out how it ties in the other victims tho.I know the Truant Officers in this area used to wear tan uniforms,&#40;don&#39;t ask,long story.lol&#41;also the 440 is an area code in OHIO.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3630-->
<!--Post: 3898--><!--Time: 1105407138-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3898"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Wichitaboy<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Wichitaboy<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, January 10, 2005 - 5:32 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->You also have fake id in the puzzle.  But when you read it backwards it could be Die KAF.  Who has initials of KAF? <BR> <BR>Mark<!--/Text--></td></tr>
